/* ───────── 4.68 BREACH (Colleen, 2026-10-19): a class game for 2-5 color crews. Each crew votes for its DECODER; the others are AGENTS.
   Every crew guards its own password: five sections of letter-number-letter-number (m2m4-b9p1-a3n5-v3c4-j2k5; 9/F and 6/W never mix).
   Everyone earns power (⚡) by answering. Agents spend it walking their crew's network map; a data cache holds one piece of a RIVAL crew's
   password, which the hand signs (the crew's COLOR, the section by LISTING, then the set) only to the agent who opened it; the agent relays it
   across the room and the decoder types it into the crew logbook (rival colors x five sections). Decoys hold fake pieces. The decoder spends
   power to CHANGE a section of the crew's own password (every rival's box for it is crossed out; the caches for it come back), to VERIFY a
   box, or to PING the nearest real cache. Trying a password freezes the whole class: the projector spotlights it, each section lights green or
   red: ACCESS GRANTED or ACCESS DENIED. The teacher chooses: the first crack ends the game, or keep going for 2nd and 3rd (the cracked crew gets
   a new password and keeps playing). ───────── */
/* 4.92 (Colleen: "breach should have a 'til someone wins' mode since i have no realistic idea of how long this should take"): an untimed game runs
   until a crew cracks a password (or the podium fills, or the teacher ends it); the clocks count UP. 3 hours is only a safety net. */
var BR_UNTIMED = 3 * 3600000;
/* 4.95 (Colleen: "a moment before the breach game starts where each team raises their hands so they can look around the room to see who's on
   their team with names for each list"): FIND YOUR CREW, one crew at a time, BR_MEET each: that crew raises their hands while everyone looks. */
var BR_MEET = 8000;
var BR_GLOW = 20000, BR_GLOW_EVERY = [180000, 240000];      // 4.68 (Colleen): a short Color Scan now and then: caches glow their crew's color
var BR_L = 'abcdefghiklmnopqrstuvwxy', BR_N = '0123456789', BR_PER = 3, BR_COST = { move: 1, wall: 3, change: 5, verify: 4, ping: 3 }, BR_FREEZE = 120000;
/* 4.91 (Colleen: "also accept passwords with a letter letter, number number pattern… the teacher chooses… practice single digit and up to quadruple
   digit numbers. only use 1 letter with triple and quadruple digit numbers"; "as long as each box is formulated the same way"): every section of
   every password in a game has the same pattern. lnln = letter, digit, letter, digit (a3n5; the digits signed one by one). The others are letters
   then ONE WHOLE NUMBER, signed as a number: lln = kr4 (0-9), llnn = kr47 (10-99), lnnn = k472 (100-999), lnnnn = k4721 (1000-9999). */
